@Tutorial(time: 35) {
    @Intro(title: "Bring the Aquarium to Life") {
        Add movement and sound to the still scene: bubbles rising from the
        seahorse, a swimming motion that sways its fins, and a sound you'll
        use later.

        This chapter comes down to one thing. **You don't write a single line
        of code here.** Particles, animation, and audio setup are all done in
        Reality Composer Pro. `AquariumView.swift` stays exactly as you wrote
        it in Chapter 1.

        You can check this chapter **in the simulator** too.

        @Image(source: "02-intro", alt: "The aquarium scene with bubbles rising")
    }

    @Section(title: "Make Bubbles with a Particle Emitter") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A **particle emitter** is a component that keeps spraying out
            small particles, used for effects like sparks, snow, and smoke.
            Here, small beads floating upward stand in for bubbles under
            water.

            You attach the emitter to **the seahorse itself** rather than a
            separate entity. That way the bubbles follow the seahorse as it
            swims, so it feels like it's cutting through water, and they come
            along when you move or duplicate it, leaving nothing extra to
            manage.

            @Image(source: "02-section1", alt: "The RCP particle emitter inspector")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In RCP, open `AquariumContent` and select `Seahorse` in the
                hierarchy panel. Add a **Particle Emitter** from
                **Add Component** at the bottom of the inspector on the right.

                Even with the default values, you can see particles spraying
                out in the viewport right away.

                @Image(source: "02-s1-step1", alt: "Adding a Particle Emitter component to Seahorse")
            }

            @Step {
                Adjust the values so it looks like bubbles. Leave
                **Emitter Shape** at `Cylinder` and lower **Birth Rate**
                (particles per second) to around `20`.

                The seahorse is small, so with the default values the particles
                cover its whole body.

                @Image(source: "02-s1-step2", alt: "Emitter Shape and Birth Rate settings")
            }

            @Step {
                Set the Y value of **Acceleration** to `1` so the particles
                float up, and change **Opacity Over Life** to
                `Gradual Fade In Out` so they appear and disappear naturally.

                @Image(source: "02-s1-step3", alt: "Acceleration Y and Opacity Over Life settings")
            }

            @Step {
                Save (**Cmd+S**) and run the app again.

                **Bubbles appear without changing a single line of code.** The
                component you attached in RCP is saved in `Scene.usda`, and
                when `Entity(named:in:)` loads the scene, the component comes
                along with it.

                @Image(source: "02-s1-step4", alt: "Bubbles rising in the simulator")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Make the Seahorse Swim with a Timeline") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            `seahorse.usdz` already contains an animation that sways its body.
            You play that animation with an RCP **Timeline**, and use a
            **Behavior** to make it "start automatically when it appears in
            the scene." No code needed here either.

            Three new ideas come up.

            - **Animation Library**: the list of animations an entity can
              use. When you add a model that contains USD animation, RCP
              attaches this component for you.
            - **Timeline**: an editor for placing actions in the order and at
              the moments you want. This one has a single action for now, but
              in Chapter 3 you chain several together.
            - **Behavior**: the trigger that decides "when to play this
              timeline."

            The butterfly that keeps flapping its wings in Apple's official
            sample uses exactly this structure — one timeline that repeats
            forever plus one `On Added To Scene` behavior.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select `Seahorse` in the hierarchy panel and look at the
                inspector. The **Animation Library** component is **already
                attached.** RCP added it automatically because
                `seahorse.usdz` contains USD animation. You can see a few
                default animations in the list.

                @Image(source: "02-s2-step1", alt: "The Animation Library component in the Seahorse inspector")
            }

            @Step {
                Go to the **Timelines** tab in the bottom panel and click
                **Create Timeline**. Double-tap the new timeline and rename it
                `SwimLoop`.

                @Image(source: "02-s2-step2", alt: "Creating the SwimLoop timeline")
            }

            @Step {
                Drag the **Animation** action from the action list on the
                right onto the timeline.

                @Image(source: "02-s2-step3", alt: "Dragging the Animation action onto the timeline")
            }

            @Step {
                When you drop it, an **Animation** action clip appears on the
                timeline. Select the clip to open the inspector.

                @Image(source: "02-s2-step3b", alt: "The Animation action clip placed on the timeline")
            }

            @Step {
                Now decide when this timeline starts. Select `Seahorse` and
                add **Behaviors** from **Add Component**.

                @Image(source: "02-s2-step4", alt: "Searching for Behaviors in Add Component")
            }

            @Step {
                In the inspector, set **Target** to `Seahorse`. The Animation
                list has two entries marked `Default`
                (`default scene animation` and `default subtree animation`);
                choose **`default scene animation`**. Then turn on
                **Repeat Forever**.

                Without Repeat Forever, it wiggles once and stops. In the
                saved file this setting shows up as `loopCount = 0`, where
                **0 means "repeat forever."** Apple's butterfly sample uses
                the same value. You can preview it with the play button at the
                top of the timeline.

                @Image(source: "02-s2-step4b", alt: "Target and Repeat Forever settings for the Animation action")
            }

            @Step {
                Set the trigger to **On Added To Scene**, set the timeline to
                run to `SwimLoop`, and save (**Cmd+S**). It means "play
                SwimLoop the moment this seahorse appears on screen."

                Run it in the simulator. The seahorse swims, and you haven't
                changed a single character of `AquariumView.swift`.

                > Note: This "zero lines of code" holds while there is **only
                > one** seahorse. If you make more with `clone(recursive:)`,
                > the behavior doesn't come along to the copies, and things
                > change. There's only one for now and it works, so move on.

                @Image(source: "02-s2-step4c", alt: "Behaviors wired up with On Added To Scene and SwimLoop")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Prepare the Sound") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            You'll make a whale call play when you tap the seahorse. For now,
            you put that sound on the scene.

            This is where things part ways with the previous two sections.
            **The particles and the animation worked the moment you attached
            their components, but sound doesn't.** Spraying continuously is a
            natural default for particles, and you gave the animation a
            "start when it appears" trigger, but the sound doesn't have a
            **when** yet.

            In Chapter 3 you make that "when" a **tap**. This section only
            prepares the ingredients.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Check that the `WhaleCry.usdz` you got in Chapter 1 is inside
                `AquariumContent.rkassets/`. If it isn't, add it now.

                > Note: It may look odd that audio has a `.usdz` extension,
                > but RealityKit handles sound in a USD container too. Drag it
                > into the RCP hierarchy and it's recognized as a playable
                > resource.

                @Image(source: "02-s3-step1", alt: "WhaleCry.usdz in the rkassets folder")
            }

            @Step {
                Select `Seahorse` and add **Audio Library** from
                **Add Component**. Then drag `WhaleCry.usdz` into this
                component's resource list, check that it's registered, and
                save (**Cmd+S**).

                **Audio Library** is "the list of sounds this entity can
                make." Once a sound is registered here, a timeline's audio
                action can pick it by name. If you run it now, there's still
                no sound. **That's expected.** You've only put the ingredient
                in place without a trigger. That's the end of Chapter 2, and
                **you haven't changed a single character of code.** RCP takes
                care of "what it looks like and what it can do," and code
                takes care of "what to do and when."

                @Image(source: "02-s3-step2", alt: "Adding an Audio Library component to Seahorse")
            }
        }
    }
}
